### bits.py
==============================================================================
1) How many states remain reachable once 2:4 sparsity is enforced
==============================================================================
  all ternary blocks of 4:                3^4 = 81
    with 0 non-zeros:                      1  (C(4,0)*2^0)
    with 1 non-zeros:                      8  (C(4,1)*2^1)
    with 2 non-zeros:                     24  (C(4,2)*2^2)
  valid X-Ternary blocks (<=2 non-zero):  33
  entropy under a uniform distribution:   log2(33)/4 = 1.2611 bit/weight

==============================================================================
2) Information content versus what is actually stored
==============================================================================
  scheme                                 needs   stores    waste   note
  BitNet b1.58 (dense ternary)          1.585b   1.585b   +0.0%   5 weights per 8 bits
  X-Ternary: information content        1.261b   2.000b  +58.6%   2 bit/weight per README
  X-Ternary + FP8 scale per 32          1.261b   2.250b  +78.4%   paper section 2.2

  X-Ternary carries 20.4% LESS information than BitNet
  and stores it in 42.0% MORE bits (with the FP8 scale).
  The paper's premise ('BitNet wastes space') is therefore inverted.

==============================================================================
3) The README's 'four states': how many are functionally distinct
==============================================================================
  01 -> +1 | 10 -> -1 | 00 -> 0 | 11 -> x
  theory.md: 'state x is defined as a Structural Zero ... corresponds to High-Z'
  In y = Wx: '00 -> ignore', '11 -> physically skip the operation'.
  Both contribute zero to y, so a 4-symbol code carries 3 values.
  Code capacity: 2 bits. Used: log2(3) = 1.5850 bits.
  Wasted by construction: 0.4150 bit/weight = 20.8% of the 2-bit space.

==============================================================================
4) Empirical entropy of what the published code actually emits
==============================================================================
  gaussian weights     blocks used  33/33  entropy 1.0890 bit/weight  non-zero 47.5%
                       -> stored at 2.0 bit/weight, waste +83.7%
  laplacian weights    blocks used  33/33  entropy 1.1695 bit/weight  non-zero 45.2%
                       -> stored at 2.0 bit/weight, waste +71.0%

### roofline.py
torch 2.5.1+cu121  |  NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090  |  VRAM 23.6 GiB

==============================================================================
1) Measured read bandwidth
==============================================================================
  read 2.0 GiB per pass, best 882 GB/s
  catalogue bandwidth 936 GB/s  =>  efficiency 94%

==============================================================================
2) Token-rate ceiling for a 70B model on one card
==============================================================================
  format                                       GiB  ceil @spec  ceil @measured
  FP16                                       130.4       6.7/s           6.3/s
  BitNet b1.58 (5 weights / 8 bits)           13.0      66.9/s          63.0/s
  X-Ternary 2 bit/weight per README           16.3      53.5/s          50.4/s
  X-Ternary + FP8 scale per 32 (sec. 2.2)     18.3      47.6/s          44.8/s
  2:4 compressed: 2x2b value + 4b index       16.3      53.5/s          50.4/s
  2:4 compressed, best case: 2x1b + 4b        12.2      71.3/s          67.2/s

  The ceiling assumes 100% bus utilisation, an empty KV cache, no
  activation traffic and no dequantization overhead. Real engines reach
  60-80% of it.
  The paper claims 60 tok/s. The format it specifies (2.25 bit/weight)
  has a ceiling of 47.6/s.

  Note: 2:4 compression saves no memory. Two surviving values at 2 bits
  plus 4 bits of index is the same 8 bits per 4 weights as dense 2-bit.
  The two ideas do not compose in the memory budget; 2:4 buys compute,
  not bandwidth.

### determinism.py
==============================================================================
1) Associativity of addition on the GPU
==============================================================================
  fp16: (a+b)+c != a+(b+c) for 31.92% of random triples
  bf16: (a+b)+c != a+(b+c) for 31.88% of random triples
  fp32: (a+b)+c != a+(b+c) for 31.86% of random triples
  => non-associativity is already in the hardware. It is not a property
     that can be added.

==============================================================================
2) Does a GEMM result depend on the reduction schedule (i.e. on batch)?
==============================================================================
  batch    2: bit-identical = True    max abs diff 0.000e+00  (0.00e+00 rel.)
  batch    8: bit-identical = True    max abs diff 0.000e+00  (0.00e+00 rel.)
  batch   64: bit-identical = False   max abs diff 1.000e+00  (4.69e-03 rel.)
  batch  256: bit-identical = False   max abs diff 1.000e+00  (4.69e-03 rel.)
  Same input vector, different batch size => different numbers.

==============================================================================
3) The same effect on a real model's logits
==============================================================================
  batch   2: bit-identical = False  max logit diff 0.2188  positions reordered in the top 10: 0
  batch   8: bit-identical = False  max logit diff 0.2500  positions reordered in the top 10: 4
  batch  32: bit-identical = False  max logit diff 0.1875  positions reordered in the top 10: 2
  Same prompt, same model, same GPU: the logits depend on the batch size.
  The cause is the reduction order inside the GEMM, not 'associative
  probability'. Determinism is obtained by fixing the schedule, not by
  adding more non-associativity.

